---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { HELIUM_URL, REPO_URL } from "../config";
---

<Base title="Privacy · rac" description="What rac never does, and everything the browser talks to.">
  <article class="frame grid gap-x-10 py-[clamp(56px,9vw,128px)] lg:grid-cols-12">
    <header class="self-start lg:sticky lg:top-10 lg:col-span-5">
      <h1 class="display-xl">Privacy</h1>
      <p class="text-lede mt-8 max-w-[34ch]">
        A browser sees everything you do online. rac is built so that none of it ever reaches rac.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose-rac mt-14 lg:col-span-6 lg:col-start-7 lg:mt-3">
      <h2>What rac never does</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>rac never sees, keeps, or sells your data. There are no rac accounts, no rac servers, no ads, and no sponsored content.</li>
        <li>rac collects no telemetry or usage statistics.</li>
        <li>A feature you turn off does no work and makes no network calls.</li>
        {/* AI wording needs Core sign-off (G7). */}
        <li>
          AI is off by default. While it’s off, none of it runs and nothing is sent. The only signs of it are the
          switch to turn it on in Settings › Assistant and one step in the welcome tour. If you turn it on, your chosen provider’s terms apply between
          you and that provider, and rac never sees your conversations. If you also let it use the browser, it
          asks before sending, buying, deleting, signing in, or typing words you didn’t write on sites you didn’t
          name, and before it takes what it read on one site to another site.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Everything the browser talks to</h2>
      <p>
        Out of the box, rac contacts exactly one host: github.com, to check for updates. Turn updates off and it
        contacts nothing. This isn’t only a promise: a release build is launched on a fresh profile, every host it
        reaches is recorded, and the <a class="text-link" href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/docs/network.md`}>list is public</a>.
      </p>
      <p>Besides the websites you visit, rac connects to three things, and only the first is on by default:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>rac’s update feed</strong>, a file on rac’s GitHub releases page that rac checks for new versions.
          You can turn update checks off on the About rac page.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Helium’s services</strong>, for extension downloads, ad-block filter lists, spell check, bangs, and
          component updates. They’re run by <a class="text-link" href={HELIUM_URL}>Helium</a>, labeled as Helium’s,
          and used only after you agree to them in the welcome tour when you first open rac. That step links to
          Helium’s privacy policy and terms.
        </li>
        <li>
          {/* AI wording needs Core sign-off (G7). */}
          <strong>Your AI provider</strong>, only after you turn AI on: the API, local model, or installed agent you
          picked. Link summaries, also only with AI on, load the linked page over your connection without your
          cookies, then send its text to your provider. Agents like Claude Code and Codex may download their
          connector from npm the first time you use them.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Crash reports</h2>
      <p>
        None. rac sends no crash reports. When they come, they’ll be off by default, and rac will ask after each
        crash before sending that one report.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <p>
        No analytics, no cookies, no third-party scripts, and no fonts loaded from other servers. Your light or dark
        choice is saved in your own browser and never sent anywhere. If your browser allows it, the download page
        also remembers, until you close the tab, that it already started your download.
      </p>

      <p>
        Questions? Open an issue on <a class="text-link" href={`${REPO_URL}/issues`}>GitHub</a>, where you can also
        read every line of rac’s code.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
